{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrs=[json.loads(l) for l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl') if l.strip()]\nfor r in rs:\n if r['id'] in ['hyp-20261004-002-r8s1','hyp-20261004-002-r9s1']:\n  print(r['id']);h=r['hypothesis'];print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r8s1\nRepartition execution as IF -> ID -> OF -> EX -> MEM -> WB, introducing\none operand-forwarding register before the integer, branch and multiply\nexecution logic. This is one structural pipeline retiming hypothesis:\nsource lookup/selection belongs to OF, arithmetic and branch resolution\nread only OF/EX registers, and division is launched from OF when possible\nwith its final arithmetic registered directly at EX/MEM publication.\nKeep single issue and nret=1. No cache or prediction-policy change is\nincluded.\n\nID registers instruction, PC, immediate, validated controls, source\naddresses, destination, prediction and validity. Move architectural RF\nreads to OF, addressed by those registered source addresses, and capture\nsource values only on actual OF acceptance. A held OF instruction must\nreread the current RF and current valid bypass sources; it must never\ndepend on a raw value captured before an older WB write. Preserve RF\nwrite-first and x0 semantics. OF/EX stores both resolved source values\nand their RVFI metadata; EX uses those registered values without a live\nforwarding mux or live dmem connection to its ALU, multiplier, comparator\nor JALR adder.\n\nOF resolves each source in youngest-writer order: the current accepted\nEX instruction's actual result, then the valid MEM instruction's\nregistered ALU result or aligned/sign-extended live load, then WB,\nthen RF. Expose EX's pre-register writeback result for this path,\nincluding PC+4 links and completed division. A back-to-back ALU or\nmultiply dependency forwards EX result into the OF/EX operand register\nat the same edge that writes EX/MEM; no extra dependency bubble is\nrequired. Suppress producers that are invalid, illegal, misaligned,\nnonwriters or rd=x0. A load in EX cannot forward data yet: interlock\nonly when the valid OF instruction actually uses its nonzero rd,\nhold ID/OF and fetch-head consumption, and insert an OF/EX validity\nbubble while that load advances to MEM. On the next accepting edge,\ncapture the correct MEM load value. Use decoded a\n…[10926 of 13998 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c08fe592f084e71a]…\nithout stalls and with --istall/--dstall. Its canonical 2K, ten-iteration,\n-O3 CoreMark run under those stalls produced all five required CRCs,\nthe validation literal, EBREAK completion, no out-of-bounds access,\nand start/stop markers at cycles 14,584 and 4,039,157. Their difference\nis exactly 4,024,573 cycles, equal to the champion, so the clock-based\nfitness projection uses a measured unchanged iteration/cycle rate.\nThese are scratch results; full formal was not run, and the final\nimplemented circuit must still pass every unchanged evaluator gate.\nConfidence is medium because the measured margin above the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold is less than one percentage\npoint and other execution/control paths now limit frequency.\n\nAcceptance requires fitness above 153.52142 iter/sec; at unchanged\ncycles median Fmax must exceed approximately 61.785 MHz. Require actual\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark cycles, canonical CRCs/UART and every unchanged\ncorrectness gate, and median Gowin timing for the final implementation.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
