LU. Its 5.442 MHz Fmax strongly\\nsuggests that the deeply unrolled divide/remainder arithmetic dominates\\nfitness. alu.sv currently expresses signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder separately. Improving this timed arithmetic cone can increase\\nCoreMark iterations/second without changing instruction throughput or\\nadding pipeline control under the approximately 22% memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothes\n…[25737 of 28809 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1fff21529f2dbc1d]…\n (+51.31%).\\n- Marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles: 4,491,485 for 10 iterations, exactly\\n  matching the prior champion. Iterations/cycle: 10 / 4,491,485.\\n- Fitness: 108.26 iterations/second, versus 71.55 before (+51.31%).\\n- Logic: 2,822; registers: 643; LUT-RAM: 8; BSRAM: 6; DSP: 6.\\n- Placement option 0's worst divider path is divisor_q -> remainder_q,\\n  27.286 ns and 109 cells, versus 36.356 ns and 249 cells before. It is\\n  now fourth in that placement's setup report. The worst overall path\\n  is register-file -> ID/EX through forwarding/control, 27.314 ns.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nNo functional issue remains from local checks. The full arithmetic\\nformal-deep suite was not run; arithmetic was checked by the independent\\ndivider oracle, the combinational ALU suite, pipeline models, and cosim.\\nForwarding/control and routing now compete with the shorter divider path\\nfor the clock limit, so further divider folding alone may have less gain.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-04T14:29:35.274725Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
