{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n j=json.loads(line)\n if j['id'] in ['hyp-20261004-001-r4s0','hyp-20261004-002-r4s1','hyp-20261004-001-r5s0']:\n  h=j.get('hypothesis')\n  print('\\\\n',j['id'], j['title'],j['fitness'],j.get('cycles'),j.get('critical_path'))\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print(h.get('motivation',''))\n   print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  else: print(h)\n  print(j.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261004-001-r4s0 Shorten five-bit divider groups with mixed-radix parallel trials 120.94 4287652 {'from': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -15.069, 'arrival_ns': 25.363, 'logic_cells': 18, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nThe current champion scores 113.92 at 48.843 MHz, and its reported worst\npath runs from quotient_q to remainder_q inside div_unit: 25.763 ns and\n162 cells. Each normal RUN clock cascades five full-width restoring\nsubtract-and-select steps. The earlier micro_opt radix-4 attempt\nhyp-20261004-001-r2s0 increased Fmax from its 32.136 MHz parent to\n42.346 MHz, but did not beat the competing five-bit scheduling change.\nIt used four radix-4 steps per clock, unlike the shorter mixed-radix\ngroup proposed here. The current seven-clock schedule and isolated\nmultiplier operands provide a better parent for optimizing just the\ndivider recurrence, without adding CoreMark cycles or another bypass.\n\nRewrite only the real arithmetic inside div_unit while retaining its\nlaunch, six five-bit RUN groups, final two-bit RUN group with fused\nsign restoration, and held DONE/consume behavior. Replace each normal\nfive-bit group with two radix-4 restoring digit steps followed by one\nradix-2 restoring bit step. This consumes exactly five dividend bits\nand produces exactly five quotient bits, but reduces serial full-width\narithmetic stages from five to three. Use the first radix-4 step alone\nfor the final group, since exactly two dividend bits remain after the\nsix normal groups. Keep seven RUN clocks in real and ALTOPS builds;\nthis is a combinational datapath optimization within the existing\nregisters, not a change in pipeline topology or instruction latency.\n\nAt accepted launch, capture unsigned divisor magnitude D and its\nwidened multiples 2D and 3D. Use explicitly sized 34-bit values;\nform 3D from zero-extended D plus 2D at launch and register it, so\nRUN never includes multiple generation. Each radix-4 step forms the\n34-bit trial T = {rem\n…[13239 of 16311 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:667b4fa2ac85e1e5]…\nonical CRC/UART checks and MMIO markers.\n\nExtend independent integer quotient/remainder vectors to negative\npartial remainders, every pair of quotient bits, all five-bit group\nboundaries, terminal correction and signed triple residues wrapping\nat 2^32 and 2^33. Retain exact seven-clock completion, operand capture,\ndelayed consumption, reset in every phase and back-to-back requests in\nreal and ALTOPS builds. Run unchanged formal, EX/pipeline unit tests,\nstalled cosim and canonical CoreMark gates. Measure the median Gowin\nFmax and real bracketed cycles; the unchanged-cycle acceptance threshold\nis Fmax>52.668192 MHz, or fitness>132.15164 iter/sec. Expect eight percent\nfitness improvement with medium confidence. The measured refined\n54.419 MHz median clears the frequency threshold at unchanged cycles;\nthe predicted fitness is about 136.54 iter/sec. Confirm bracketed cycles,\nformal and CRC gates in evaluation. Integer/load forwarding is now\nthe measured timing limit, and synthesis/placement sensitivity remains.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
