ongly\\nsuggests that the deeply unrolled divide/remainder arithmetic dominates\\nfitness. alu.sv currently expresses signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder separately. Improving this timed arithmetic cone can increase\\nCoreMark iterations/second without changing instruction throughput or\\nadding pipeline control under the approximately 22% memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothes\n…[100340 of 103412 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:72a51f7dfac007bb]…\nn- `python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options`: all placements pass.\\n  Options 0/1/2 report 50.431 / 50.384 / 50.384 MHz. Median: 50.384 MHz.\\n  Option-0 area: 3,615 logic, 905 registers, 8 LUTRAM, 6 BSRAM, 6 DSP.\\n  Its worst path is inside the unchanged divider at 25.123 ns; options 1/2\\n  likewise remain divider-limited at approximately 25.142 ns.\\n- Fitness from the measured median and marker cycles:\\n  `50.384e6 * 10 / 3829487 = 131.568536`, or 131.57 iter/s.\\n  This is below the required 140.78114 (approximately 53.914 MHz at unchanged\\n  cycles), so this hypothesis is a performance regression, not an improvement.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nNo correctness failure remains in the completed local gates. Removing the\\nlive comparator from queue/decode controls did not improve the scored median\\nFmax: the unchanged divider remains critical, and placement changed its\\ndelay. No unrelated datapath optimization was attempted to compensate.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-04T17:09:17.236260Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
