{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261004-001-r2s0','hyp-20261004-001-r4s0','hyp-20261004-002-r4s1','hyp-20261004-001-r5s0','hyp-20261004-001-r6s0','hyp-20261004-001-r8s0'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  print('\\\\nID:',d['id'],'\\\\nTITLE:',d['title'],'\\\\nMOTIVATION:',d['motivation'],'\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:',h['hypothesis'] if isinstance(h,dict) else h,'\\\\nNOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: hyp-20261004-001-r2s0 \nTITLE: Shorten the registered divider with four parallel-trial radix-4 steps \nMOTIVATION: The current champion scores 71.55 at 32.136 MHz. Its worst vendor-timed\npath is 36.356 ns across 249 cells, entirely within u_ex/u_div, from a\ndivisor register to a remainder register. The engine currently cascades\neight dependent 33-bit restoring subtract/select steps in each RUN\nclock. This arithmetic chain limits the clock for every instruction.\nThe earlier fully combinational radix-4 attempt still had sixteen\ndependent digit steps and gained only 9.90% over the original baseline;\nthis proposal instead optimizes the champion's existing registered\nengine, which has only eight quotient bits to compute per clock.\nReducing its dependent arithmetic depth without adding cycles directly\ntargets the measured bottleneck and preserves the fixed liveness bound.\n \nHYPOTHESIS: Replace the eight radix-2 restoring steps in each div_unit RUN clock\nwith four radix-4 steps, each producing two quotient bits. Preserve the\nexisting launch, four RUN clocks, one SIGN clock, and held DONE result\nschedule exactly in both real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds. This is a\nlocal arithmetic rewrite: the pipeline and divider handshake keep their\ncurrent behavior, and CoreMark instruction throughput should stay the\nsame while the divider's register-to-register path becomes shorter.\n\nAt launch capture the unsigned divisor magnitude D and the widened\nmultiples 2D and 3D. All multiples must be 34 bits; form 3D with an\nexplicitly widened addition during launch rather than recomputing it\ninside the RUN recurrence. Each digit step forms the 34-bit trial\nT = {remainder[31:0], quotient[31:30]}. Compute T-D, T-2D, and T-3D in\nparallel as 35-bit zero-extended subtractions. Their bit 34 supplies\nborrow, so no separate compare followed by subtract is needed. With\nok1, ok2, and ok3 denoting no-borrow for D, 2D, and 3D respectively,\nappend the quotient digit {ok2, ok3 | (ok1 & !ok2)}. Select the low\n32 remainder bits using disjoint masks !ok\n…[25490 of 28562 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7994df8c9808328b]…\nted\nCoreMark cycles, CRCs/UART and median Gowin Fmax under the existing\nstalls. Parallel trials increase LUT area and divisor fanout; the\nlaunch and live-load/ALU paths can cap frequency. A temporary implementation with this digit order and the separate\nterminal path passed full-core Verilator lint and the existing real\nand ALTOPS divider tests and\nmeasured 51.069/53.299/53.299 MHz across Gowin placement options, median\n53.299 MHz, using 4,059 LUTs and 1,004 FFs. This is a local timing probe,\nnot a completed formal/cosim/CoreMark evaluation. The implied clock gain\nis about 3.41 percent and does not yet clear the acceptance margin;\nat unchanged cycles this projects roughly 139.18 iter/sec. The remaining\nworst paths cross memory, integer forwarding and ALU logic. The forecast\ntherefore remains low-confidence rather than claiming an accepted result. At unchanged cycles acceptance\nrequires fitness above 140.78114 iter/sec and median Fmax above about\n53.914 MHz, beyond the 4.6 percent acceptance margin.\n \nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
