{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('id') in ['hyp-20261004-002-r4s1','hyp-20261004-002-r5s1','hyp-20261004-002-r6s1','hyp-20261004-002-r7s1']:\n  h=r['hypothesis']\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r['fitness'],r['fmax_mhz'],r['cycles'],r['critical_path'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r4s1 Retimed radix-4 divider with three digit stages and registered multiples 122.81 52.657 4287652 {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -13.683, 'arrival_ns': 23.978, 'logic_cells': 12, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nRestructure only the shared multicycle divider into one preparation\nclock, five clocks that each process three radix-4 digits (six dividend\nbits), and one terminal clock that processes a single radix-4 digit\n(two bits) and restores the result sign. This consumes exactly 32 bits\nin seven RUN clocks, preserving the existing completion schedule and\nall pipeline boundaries. The recurring datapath has three dependent\nsubtract/select stages instead of five; the terminal path has one\ndigit stage followed by sign restoration.\n\nAt the accepted launch edge L, capture the dividend magnitude, divisor\nmagnitude D, operation and sign flags exactly as today. Do not compute\n3D on the launch path: it already includes forwarding and potentially\nlive load data. At edge L+1, use the captured D to register the unsigned\n34-bit value 3D = zero_extend(D) + (zero_extend(D) << 1), while retaining\nquotient and remainder unchanged. D and 2D are explicitly widened to\n34 bits from the captured divisor; 2D is a wiring shift. All RUN digit\narithmetic uses these registered sources, never live operands.\n\nReuse one physical chain of three radix-4 digit cells on edges L+2\nthrough L+6. Each cell forms the 34-bit unsigned trial\nT = {remainder[31:0], quotient[31:30]} and computes T-D, T-2D and T-3D\nin parallel as explicitly zero-extended 35-bit subtractions. Bit 34\nindicates borrow; do not implement a comparison followed by a second\nsubtraction. Let ok1, ok2 and ok3 be the three no-borrow predicates.\nAppend quotient digit {ok2, ok3 | (ok1 & !ok2)}. Select the low 32 bits\nof the next remainder using disjoint masks !ok1, ok1 & !ok2,\nok2 & !ok3 and ok3 on T and the respective differences, expressed as\na parallel masked OR rather than\n…[14437 of 17509 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7ae516482081ac97]…\ngnitude. Keep quotient negation\ndisabled for zero divisor, dividend-only remainder sign restoration,\nDIV(INT_MIN,-1)=INT_MIN and REM(INT_MIN,-1)=0. Preserve rejected starts,\nsimultaneous consume/relaunch, reset cancellation and stable DONE results.\nKeep EX operand/RVFI capture, single publication, memory backpressure,\nheld-WB forwarding, the nine-cycle consecutive-divide and ten-cycle\nredirect-to-divide retirement bounds, decoder legality, nret=1 ports,\nstrict retirement order and every existing correctness assertion.\n\nThis is one divider launch/recurrence retiming hypothesis. Require the\nunchanged formal and cosim gates, exact real-divider arithmetic tests,\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART and address bounds, and marker-bracketed\ntiming. The expected eight-percent gain assumes unchanged CoreMark\ncycles and a shorter divider path; existing RF/EX and load/control paths\nmay cap frequency. Acceptance requires fitness above 140.78114 iter/sec,\napproximately 53.914 MHz median Fmax at the champion cycle count.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
