{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Replace combinational division with a bounded multicycle EX divider\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The sole recorded experiment is the baseline: fitness 12.12, median Fmax\\n5.442 MHz, 12312 LUT4s, and a 199.837 ns path with 156 cells through\\nforwarding, EX, and the ALU. There are no prior structural attempts or\\nLESSONS.md. The ALU currently computes signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder with combinational / and % operators. These long arithmetic\\nchains constrain the clock even when the executing instruction is an\\nordinary integer operation. Sharing and registering division should\\nsubstantially improve frequency and reduce LUT pressure at the cost of\\nextra cycles only for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. Multiplication remains\\non its existing path, preserving throughput in CoreMark's matrix loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The sole recorded experiment is the baseline: fitness 12.12, median Fmax\\n5.442 MHz, 12312 LUT4s, and a 199.837 ns path with 156 cells through\\nforwarding, EX, and the ALU. There are no prior structural attempts or\\nLESSONS.md. The ALU currently computes signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder with combinational / and % operators. These long arithmetic\\nchains constrain the clock even when the executing instruction is an\\nordinary integer operation. Sharing and registering division should\\nsubstantially improve frequency and reduce LUT pressure at the cost of\\nextra cycles only for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. Multiplication remains\\non its existing path, preserving throughput in CoreMark's matrix loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational EX result path into one\\nregistered, unsigned-magnitude quotient/remainder engine. Use a restoring\\nshift/subtract recurrence with eight quotient bits computed per clock:\\neight bit steps are unrolled combinationally and reused for four clocks,\\nrather than cascading all 32 steps in one clock. Register operand\\nmagnitudes and sign flags at launch, then restore\n…[22629 of 25701 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:eb9ee7337b9e6fe7]…\n (+51.31%).\\n- Marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles: 4,491,485 for 10 iterations, exactly\\n  matching the prior champion. Iterations/cycle: 10 / 4,491,485.\\n- Fitness: 108.26 iterations/second, versus 71.55 before (+51.31%).\\n- Logic: 2,822; registers: 643; LUT-RAM: 8; BSRAM: 6; DSP: 6.\\n- Placement option 0's worst divider path is divisor_q -> remainder_q,\\n  27.286 ns and 109 cells, versus 36.356 ns and 249 cells before. It is\\n  now fourth in that placement's setup report. The worst overall path\\n  is register-file -> ID/EX through forwarding/control, 27.314 ns.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nNo functional issue remains from local checks. The full arithmetic\\nformal-deep suite was not run; arithmetic was checked by the independent\\ndivider oracle, the combinational ALU suite, pipeline models, and cosim.\\nForwarding/control and routing now compete with the shorter divider path\\nfor the clock limit, so further divider folding alone may have less gain.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-04T14:29:35.274725Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
