{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    if r.get('category') == 'structural':\n        h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n        print(r['id'],r['title'])\n        print('fitness',r.get('fitness'),'fmax',r.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',r.get('cycles'))\n        print('path',r.get('critical_path'))\n        print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n        print(r.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 Replace combinational division with a bounded multicycle EX divider\nfitness 71.55 fmax 32.136 cycles 4491485\npath {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/divisor_q_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/remainder_q_1_s0', 'slack_ns': -26.062, 'arrival_ns': 36.356, 'logic_cells': 249, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\nMove DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational EX result path into one\nregistered, unsigned-magnitude quotient/remainder engine. Use a restoring\nshift/subtract recurrence with eight quotient bits computed per clock:\neight bit steps are unrolled combinationally and reused for four clocks,\nrather than cascading all 32 steps in one clock. Register operand\nmagnitudes and sign flags at launch, then restore quotient sign from\nrs1-sign XOR rs2-sign and remainder sign from rs1. Reserve at most seven\nclocks from accepted launch to result availability, including setup and\nsign restoration; keep the zero-wait retirement gap within the unchanged\nten-cycle liveness window, including pipeline transfer cycles. Use the\nsame bounded handshake latency in ALTOPS mode, substituting only the\narithmetic value with the exact existing formal formulas. Do not hide a\nslow real divider behind a faster formal-only completion path.\n\nLaunch exactly once for a valid legal divide in ID/EX when the older MEM\ninstruction can advance. Capture the forwarded operands and their RVFI\nvalues before holding the instruction, since forwarding sources can\ndisappear while older instructions drain. Hold PC and ID/EX during the\noperation; allow older EX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions to drain once,\ninserting EX/MEM bubbles instead of repeatedly issuing the old instruction.\nExisting dmem backpressure must still hold an outstanding memory request.\nRetain a completed division result until EX/MEM accepts it, then publish\nit exactly once through the normal forwarding, writeback, and single\nchannel RVFI paths. Qualify redirects and side effects with valid and\nactual stage advance so stalls cannot replay a branch or memory request.\n\nPreserve the e\n…[7538 of 10610 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6d6083b86701ed09]…\n 0/1/2: 45.414 / 48.625 / 48.625 MHz.\n- Median Fmax: 48.625 MHz, versus 32.136 MHz before (+51.31%).\n- Marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles: 4,491,485 for 10 iterations, exactly\n  matching the prior champion. Iterations/cycle: 10 / 4,491,485.\n- Fitness: 108.26 iterations/second, versus 71.55 before (+51.31%).\n- Logic: 2,822; registers: 643; LUT-RAM: 8; BSRAM: 6; DSP: 6.\n- Placement option 0's worst divider path is divisor_q -> remainder_q,\n  27.286 ns and 109 cells, versus 36.356 ns and 249 cells before. It is\n  now fourth in that placement's setup report. The worst overall path\n  is register-file -> ID/EX through forwarding/control, 27.314 ns.\n\n## Concerns\n\nNo functional issue remains from local checks. The full arithmetic\nformal-deep suite was not run; arithmetic was checked by the independent\ndivider oracle, the combinational ALU suite, pipeline models, and cosim.\nForwarding/control and routing now compete with the shorter divider path\nfor the clock limit, so further divider folding alone may have less gain.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
