{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r3s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Separate pipeline validity from payload storage to shorten branch feedback\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 108.26 iterations/second at 48.625 MHz with 4,491,485\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Both prior\n  structural attempts registered and folded the divider; the latest\n  implementation notes identify forwarding/control as the new competing\n  bottleneck. Its reported worst path is 27.314 ns across 48 cells, from\n  register-file storage through ID, EX, IF, hazards, and back into ID/EX.\n  Currently a resolved redirect replaces fetched instruction bits with a\n  NOP before decode and hazard comparisons, while ID/EX flush clears the\n  entire packed payload. This couples late branch resolution to operand\n  addressing, decoding, load-use arbitration, and many payload registers.\n  A separate validity plane can remove these dependencies without adding\n  a recovery cycle or changing the seven-clock divider schedule.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure pipeline storage as independently controlled payload and\n  validity registers. Keep the existing stage boundaries and EX branch\n  resolution cycle. A bubble or branch squash clears only stage validity;\n  payload storage is never cleared or selected by redirect, load-use\n  bubble, or an unavailable fetch. This is one structural change to the\n  pipeline control topology, with unchanged arithmetic and instruction\n  scheduling.\n\n  IF must present the raw imem instruction and PC to decode regardless of\n  redirect or io_imemReady. Remove the NOP substitution mux. Represent\n  fetch availability with a separate bit based on io_imemReady, and apply\n  the accepted EX redirect as a late kill of the younger ID/EX valid bit.\n  Source addresses, immediate generation, and decoded payload bits must\n  have no combinational dependency on redirect. The PC still gives an\n  accepted redirect priority over every front-end stall, including an\n  imem stall. Reset remains highest priority.\n\n  Split the current stall_id/flu\n…[4535 of 7607 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:569275802a76e526]…\nvider starts with\n      accepted validity. Keep the ALU, divider arithmetic, and latency.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire raw fetch availability and distinct hold/kill controls through\n      the existing stage boundaries. Audit valid-qualified forwarding,\n      memory side effects, and retirement, retaining WB forwarding\n      provenance and every nret=1 IO/RVFI field.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed taken-branch/JAL/JALR squashes whose wrong-path words\n      encode stores, divides, loads, and nonzero register writes. Exercise\n      stale instructions during imem stalls, overlapping backend holds,\n      load-use recovery, held-WB forwarding, and reset with stale payloads.\n      Check exact retired PC/source/result/order values, no wrong-path\n      side effects, one-cycle redirect/load-use penalties, and unchanged\n      divide and redirect-to-divide liveness gaps under zero-wait and\n      directed/random memory stalls. Preserve all existing assertions.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
