{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor n,line in enumerate(Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines(),1):\n if n in (14,17):\n  d=json.loads(line)\n  print(d['id'], d['title'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n  print(d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-001-r5s0 Speculative non-restoring pairs with parallel signed divisor preparation\nReplace only div_unit's real restoring arithmetic with signed partial\nremainders and speculative pairs of non-restoring radix-2 steps.\nPreserve six five-bit groups, the final two-bit group, seven RUN clocks,\nall launch/consume semantics and the existing CPU pipeline. Each normal\ngroup uses two speculative two-bit cells followed by one ordinary\nnon-restoring bit cell; the terminal group uses the first pair alone.\nThis changes local combinational arithmetic and a few divider registers,\nwithout changing any instruction's latency or stall behavior.\n\nCapture unsigned divisor magnitude D and a 33-bit two's-complement\n-D at launch. Use a signed 33-bit partial remainder R initialized to\nzero, so all unsigned divisors through 0xffffffff are representable.\nOne non-restoring step with dividend bit b computes\nR1=2R+b+(R<0 ? D : -D), and emits quotient bit !(R1<0).\nKeep the recurrence in explicitly sized 33-bit modular arithmetic.\nFor D nonzero its selected result stays in [-D,D), so bit 32 is the\ncorrect sign even when an intermediate shifted value wraps.\n\nFor a pair with incoming bits b1,b0 let S=4R+2*b1+b0 modulo 2^33.\nIn parallel compute the ordinary first-step R1 and both possible\nsecond-step results:\n  N = S + (R<0 ? 3D : -D);\n  P = S + (R<0 ? D : -3D).\nSelect R2 = R1<0 ? N : P and append the two quotient bits\n{!(R1<0),!(R2<0)}. Derive both N and P directly from incoming R and\nthe two dividend bits, not by doubling the just-computed R1.\nThe first-step sign and two second-step candidates therefore traverse\nparallel carry chains. A pair has one dependent arithmetic depth plus\nselection, rather than two serial arithmetic depths. Use precomputed\n+/-D and +/-3D registers and two candidate adders per pair; avoid\nexpanding into four complete second-step adders, which increases\nrouting without an observed timing advantage.\n\nPrepare +/-3D directly from the forwarded raw divisor b in parallel\nwith magnitude capture. Define s=signed_op&&\n…[5104 of 8176 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:65957d0158cef010]…\nigned remainder follows only the\ndividend, and INT_MIN/-1 yields INT_MIN quotient and zero remainder.\nKeep the ALTOPS formulas and identical seven-clock completion schedule.\nKeep reset cancellation, rejected starts, simultaneous consume/start,\nstable DONE retention, EX operand/RVFI capture, nret=1 ports, decoder\nlegality, retirement order, liveness, and all bus-stall behavior.\n\nThe intended gain is shorter borrow and difference propagation in\nevery dependent restoring step at unchanged CoreMark cycles. The\nmain risks are duplicated block arithmetic, prefix routing and the\nexisting integer/forwarding paths becoming the clock limit. The\nestimate is not a measured fitness result. Require unchanged formal,\narithmetic unit, stalled cosim, memory-bound, CRC and UART gates,\nwith canonical marker-bracketed cycles. At unchanged cycles the\nacceptance threshold is median Gowin Fmax greater than 53.91084 MHz,\nor fitness greater than 140.78114 iter/sec; small placement variation\nalone does not establish the hypothesis.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
