{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r8s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Retiming divider completion into EX publication enables a four-step body\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  LESSONS.md is absent. The champion scores 134.59 iter/sec at 51.540 MHz\n  with 3,829,487 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its five-step restoring\n  divider competes with live load/EX paths for the clock limit. Prior\n  spatial duplication and radix-4 preparation increased routing; round\n  seven's four-bit launch prefix regressed to 42.831 MHz because its final\n  path combined four restoring steps with sign correction. The current\n  divider registers a completed result and then spends another edge\n  copying that result into EX/MEM. Retiming the last arithmetic across\n  this existing transfer boundary offers a shorter recurring path without\n  adding an execution clock or repeating the long terminal path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure the divider around its existing EX publication edge. Capture\n  a two-bit leading prefix at launch, process seven groups of four bits\n  into registered quotient/remainder state, and compute the final two bits\n  plus sign restoration combinationally from that held state into the\n  EX/MEM publication register. Exactly 2 + 7*4 + 2 = 32 dividend bits are\n  consumed. Remove the divider's intervening completed-result register:\n  the architectural result is registered by EX/MEM instead. This is one\n  structural retiming of the divider completion boundary, with a shared\n  four-cell restoring body and the unchanged CPU stage topology.\n\n  At accepted launch edge L, derive the unsigned dividend magnitude and\n  divisor magnitude and capture all existing signs and operation flags.\n  Compute divmod of only the magnitude's leading two bits P. If D exceeds\n  three, the prefix quotient is zero and its remainder is P; otherwise\n  use two narrow restoring cells with explicitly sized three-bit trial\n  subtraction. Derive the small-divisor predicate and prefix magnitude\n  directly from raw forwarded operands in parallel with full magnitudes,\n  so a second arithmetic \n…[4699 of 7771 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bde30eb9f2c3659d]…\no. Cover all\n      final quotient pairs and both signs. Preserve every seven-clock\n      result-valid assertion, held-result/input-capture assertion,\n      rejected-start, reset-phase and consume/relaunch check in real and\n      ALTOPS builds; explicitly check READY result stability.\n  - file: test/test_ex_divider.py\n    description: |\n      Verify direct final-result capture at the unchanged publication\n      edge, launch prefix from forwarded ALU/load sources, source changes\n      after launch, stalls spanning READY, captured RVFI payload, reset\n      cancellation and exactly one EX/MEM publication for each divide.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise prefix and final-pair boundaries in dependent DIV/REM\n      chains, signed/unsigned corners, x0 destinations and branch/JALR\n      targets beginning with divides under independent bus stalls.\n      Preserve exact nine/ten-cycle zero-wait progress assertions,\n      retirement order/source/result checks and memory-side-effect tests.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
