{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r9s1.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['title']='Specialize divider preparation to enable two radix-4 feedback stages'\nh['motivation']='''The six-stage champion scores 146.77 iter/sec at 59.068 MHz and\n4,024,573 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path is now\nentirely inside div_unit, remainder_q back to remainder_q: 22.272 ns\nand 128 cells. Four dependent full-width subtract/restore cells remain\non every RUN feedback path. The operand-stage change removed the live\nRF/load execution paths that limited earlier parallel-trial experiments.\n\nThe structural history has been checked. r4s1 used three radix-4 cells;\nr6s1 used a leading byte and two radix-4 cells, but both were limited by\nRF/MEM execution paths. r7s1 regressed because four restoring cells fed\nfinal sign correction. Preserve the champion's short two-bit launch\nand separate terminal publication, and specialize the first RUN edge\nfor narrow prefix arithmetic plus divisor preparation. Subsequent RUN\nedges can then reuse just two full-width radix-4 cells, with no added\nCPU stage or divide latency.\n'''\nh['hypothesis']='''Restructure the shared divider into three physically distinct paths:\nthe existing two-bit launch, a narrow first RUN/preparation stage, and\na two-cell radix-4 feedback body, followed by the existing independent\nheld-state terminal two-bit/sign path. Preserve accepted launch L,\nseven RUN edges L+1..L+7, READY after L+7 and EX/MEM consumption at L+8.\nThis is one structural specialization and folding hypothesis; retain the\nsix-stage CPU, fetch queue, predictor, nret=1 and all memory interfaces.\n\nKeep the exact two-bit launch-prefix logic, low-thirty-bit magnitude,\nfull divisor magnitude and captured sign/op flags. At RUN group zero,\nL+1, the input remainder has only two significant bits. Consume the\nnext four dividend bits with four narrow restoring cells. The complete\nsix-bit prefix is at most 63: if D[31:6]\"'!=0, append four quotient zeros\nand set R={old_R[1:0],old_Q[31:28]}; otherwise use D[5:0] in four\nseven-bit unsigned subtract/restore steps. Each narrow difference'\"'s\nbit six is borrow; retain six remainder bits and shift Q by one at\neach step. At this edge register Q={old_Q[27:0],new_digit} and the\nzero-extended six-bit remainder. D=0 must append four quotient ones\nand retain the prefix as remainder. Do not feed full-width multiple\npreparation into the live launch logic.\n\nAt L+1 also register explicitly widened unsigned 34-bit\n3D=zero_extend(D)+(zero_extend(D)<<1), from captured divisor_q.\nGenerate D and 2D by zero extension and wiring shifts. This preparation\nruns beside the narrow prefix stage and adds no execution clock.\nNo divisor-multiple addition belongs in the recurring RUN datapath.\n\nOn RUN groups one through six, L+2..L+7, reuse exactly two dependent\nradix-4 digit cells. Each forms unsigned 34-bit T={R,Q[31:30]} and\ncomputes T-D, T-2D and T-3D as independent zero-extended 35-bit\nsubtractions. Let ok1,ok2,ok3 be clear borrow bits. Append the digit\n{ok2, ok3 | (ok1 & \"'!ok2)}. Select the low 32 remainder bits with\nparallel disjoint masks !ok1, ok1 & !ok2, ok2 & !ok3, and ok3 on T\nand the respective differences. Use masked OR rather than cascaded\npriority selection. Cell two reads cell one'\"'s restored state; register\nonly cell two's Q/R after each full four-bit group. Preserve all high\nmultiple and trial bits until borrow detection. For nonzero D, restored\nR<D fits 32 bits; for D=0 every digit is three and the remainder\nreconstructs the dividend magnitude.\n\nAfter L+7 exactly thirty bits have been processed:\n2 launch + 4 first-RUN prefix + 6*4 feedback. Keep the existing final\ntwo-bit lookahead and parallel sign restoration physically independent\nof the feedback cells. They read held quotient_q/remainder_q and the\nregistered divisor/triple, and publish directly into EX/MEM at L+8.\nDo not feed RUN outputs or a prefix/body selection mux into terminal\narithmetic, introduce padding bits, add a result register or extend\nlatency. READY state, data and valid remain stable during delayed consume,\nchanging live inputs and rejected starts.\n\nPreserve start/busy/result_valid/consume, simultaneous consume/relaunch,\nreset cancellation, early OF launch, registered EX fallback, isolated\nearly-divider operand selection and exactly one publication. Real and\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds retain the same seven-RUN schedule and every\nexact existing subtraction/XOR formula. Preserve all RV32M results,\nzero-divisor suppression of quotient negation, dividend-only remainder\nsign, INT_MIN/-1, exact RVFI operands/order, and outstanding memory\nrequests. Retain all existing nine-cycle consecutive-divide and\nten-cycle redirect-to-divide assertions, decoder legality, nret=1 ports,\nformal/cosim/CRC/UART/address-bound/marker checks.\n\nA scratch implementation of this exact partition passed the existing\nreal and ALTOPS divider tests and EX-divider tests. Gowin placement\noptions 0/1/2 measured 60.949/62.021/62.021 MHz, median 62.021 MHz,\nusing 4,431 LUTs and 963 FFs, versus the champion's 4,292 LUTs and\n963 FFs. This is a local arithmetic/integration and timing probe,\nnot a completed formal/cosim/CoreMark fitness evaluation. At unchanged\nCoreMark cycles, it projects approximately 154.11 iter/sec, a 5.00\npercent gain. The initial larger radix-16 probe passed those unit tests\nbut reached only 57.631 MHz and 5,311 LUTs, so the selected hypothesis\nuses the smaller two-digit body. Confidence is medium: the measured\nmargin above acceptance is narrow and full-core validation remains.\n\nAcceptance requires fitness above 153.52142 iter/sec; at unchanged\ncycles median Fmax must exceed approximately 61.785 MHz. Require actual\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark cycles, canonical CRCs/UART and every unchanged\ncorrectness gate, and median Gowin timing for the final implementation.\n'''\nh['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'medium'}\nh['changes'][0]['description']='''Retain the two-bit launch prefix and seven-RUN/held-READY protocol.\nSpecialize group zero as four seven-bit narrow prefix steps in parallel\nwith registered 34-bit 3D preparation. Reuse two full-width radix-4\nparallel-trial cells for groups one through six, using explicit unsigned\nwidths and disjoint masked-OR remainder selection. Keep terminal\nheld-state two-bit/sign arithmetic independent of the body, preserving\nexact real/ALTOPS timing, corner cases and stable result retention.\n'''\nh['changes'][1]['description']='''Retain existing arithmetic, exact seven-clock valid, rejected-start,\nconsume/relaunch, reset and held-result assertions in both modes. Add\nall six-bit prefixes and radix-4 digits, divisor boundary 63/64,\nmaximum-width 3D, group-boundary and final-pair cases, with an independent\nRV32M oracle and randomized signed/unsigned operands. Verify stable\nterminal results under delayed consumption.\n'''\nh['changes'][2]['description']='''Preserve all early-launch, EX-fallback, exact publication and held-RVFI\nchecks. Exercise new prefix/divisor boundaries and large unsigned\nmultiples around READY stalls, forwarded operand capture, consecutive\ndivides, reset cancellation and one-time EX/MEM publication.\n'''\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('VALID',p)\nprint('Projected fitness:',62.021e6*10/4024573)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
