{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d['title'], 'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'outcome',d.get('outcome'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict): h=h.get('hypothesis','')\n  print(h)\n  if d.get('implementation_notes'): print('NOTES:',d['implementation_notes'])\n  print()\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 Fetch-time bimodal prediction removes taken-branch bubbles fitness 12.59 fmax 5.292 cycles 4201766 outcome regression\nAdd one compact fetch-time control-flow predictor: a 64-entry bimodal\nbranch-history table of two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[7:2]\nand reset to weakly not taken (01). Predecode only legal RV32I conditional\nbranches and JAL from the raw fetched instruction. For a conditional\nbranch, use the counter MSB as the direction; predict JAL taken without\nconsulting the table. Form direct targets from PC plus the B/J immediate,\nand predict only word-aligned targets. All other instructions, including\nJALR, predict PC+4. Keep direction state only: no BTB, global history,\nreturn stack, target cache, or instruction buffering.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF/ID and ID/EX. In EX compute the existing architecturally resolved next\nPC, including the existing sequential-next-PC behavior on alignment\ntraps. Redirect and flush the younger instruction only when this resolved\nnext PC differs from the prediction, gated by ID/EX.valid and EX acceptance\n(!stall_ex_mem). A predicted-taken branch that resolves not taken must\nredirect to its own PC+4. Correctly predicted control transfers must not\nflush or redirect. RVFI pc_wdata remains the actual resolved next PC and\nJAL/JALR link writes remain PC+4.\n\nTrain a conditional branch's indexed counter toward its actual outcome\nexactly once when the valid, legal, aligned branch advances from EX into\nEX/MEM. Never train held EX instructions, bubbles, jumps, or trapped\nbranches. Predictor lookup is combinational and updates are synchronous;\nsame-index fetch/update may use the pre-update counter, since the chosen\nnext PC is captured with the instruction. A prediction changes the fetch\nPC only when fetch/decode acceptance is enabled. Reset overrides all PC\nupdates; an accepted EX misprediction overrides fetch stall; otherwise\nload-use, imem, and dmem stalls hold the PC and preserve held prediction\nmetadata. Su\n…[45741 of 48813 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0727363235f5f36f]…\nr. Keep actual RVFI next PCs, PC+4 link values,\ndecoder default-illegal behavior, alignment traps, nret=1 IO, strict\nretirement order, stalled-WB forwarding and all bus acceptance gates.\nPredictor state may only choose fetch direction, never architectural\nresults or whether an accepted branch receives verification.\n\nThe modeled cycle change gives only 0.478 percent more iterations per\ncycle, approximately 147.47 iter/sec at the champion's 59.068 MHz.\nThe one-percent forecast is low confidence, not a claim that the\nreplay clears the 4.6-percent acceptance margin. It would need more\nthan approximately 61.492 MHz median Fmax at the modeled cycle count.\nThe current divider remains the timing limit; extra lookup/tag logic and placement changes\nmay erase cycle savings. Require unchanged unit, formal, cosim, memory\nbounds, canonical CRC/UART and marker-timing checks. Acceptance requires\nmeasured median Gowin Fmax times CoreMark iterations/cycle to exceed\n153.52142 iter/sec, the champion's 146.77 plus 4.6 percent.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
