{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID',d['id'],'TITLE',d['title'])\n  print('RESULT', {k:d.get(k) for k in ('outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','error')})\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{}); print('MOTIVATION',h.get('motivation','') if isinstance(h,dict) else d.get('motivation',''))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS',h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 TITLE Fetch-time bimodal prediction removes taken-branch bubbles\nRESULT {'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 12.59, 'delta_pct': 3.88, 'fmax_mhz': 5.292, 'cycles': 4201766, 'lut4': 13072, 'ff': 620, 'error': None}\nMOTIVATION The current core always fetches PC+4 and flushes the younger instruction\non every taken conditional branch or jump, even for stable CoreMark loop\nbranches. IF/ID is combinational, so steering the PC when an instruction\nis accepted into ID/EX can supply its predicted successor without adding\na pipeline stage. A small direction-only predictor avoids a tagged target\narray and should reduce benchmark cycles while leaving the dominant\ncombinational M-extension timing path structurally unchanged. The local\nhistory has only the baseline and no prior predictor attempts or lessons.\n\nHYPOTHESIS Add one compact fetch-time control-flow predictor: a 64-entry bimodal\nbranch-history table of two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[7:2]\nand reset to weakly not taken (01). Predecode only legal RV32I conditional\nbranches and JAL from the raw fetched instruction. For a conditional\nbranch, use the counter MSB as the direction; predict JAL taken without\nconsulting the table. Form direct targets from PC plus the B/J immediate,\nand predict only word-aligned targets. All other instructions, including\nJALR, predict PC+4. Keep direction state only: no BTB, global history,\nreturn stack, target cache, or instruction buffering.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF/ID and ID/EX. In EX compute the existing architecturally resolved next\nPC, including the existing sequential-next-PC behavior on alignment\ntraps. Redirect and flush the younger instruction only when this resolved\nnext PC differs from the prediction, gated by ID/EX.valid and EX acceptance\n(!stall_ex_mem). A predicted-taken branch that resolves not taken must\nredirect to its own PC+4. Correctly predicted control transfers must not\nflush or redirect. RVFI pc_wdata remains the actual resolved next\n…[63361 of 66433 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:390b886bf3d615c1]…\n/68.231/68.231 MHz, 4,493 LUTs and\n964 FFs. Canonical -O3, 2K, ten-iteration CoreMark with both random bus\nstalls has start/stop markers 14,584/4,039,157, exactly 4,024,573 timed\ncycles, all five canonical CRCs, the validation literal, EBREAK completion\nand no out-of-bounds access. Full-RVFI selftest cosim with stalls and all\nthree existing random programs with and without stalls pass. Verilator\n-Wall lint passes without warnings. The unchanged single-channel fast\nALTOPS formal configuration, staged in a private copy of the upstream\nframework, passes all 53 checks, including reg, liveness, unique and\nill; its definitions and depths are unchanged. These are scratch-\nprototype results; the final implementation must still\npass every unchanged correctness gate and reproduce the timing result.\nThe projected 169.54 score barely exceeds required 169.0336; at unchanged\ncycles median Fmax must exceed 68.028807 MHz. This small margin makes\nexact recovery/producer qualification and final median timing material.\n\nNOTES \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
