{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261004-003-r4s2','hyp-20261004-003-r5s2','hyp-20261004-003-r6s2','hyp-20261004-003-r7s2','hyp-20261004-003-r8s2','hyp-20261004-003-r9s2'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['id'] in ids:\n  h=r['hypothesis']\n  print('\\\\n'+r['id']+'\\\\nMOTIVATION\\\\n'+h['motivation']+'\\\\nHYPOTHESIS\\\\n'+h['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nhyp-20261004-003-r4s2\nMOTIVATION\nNo LESSONS.md exists. The earlier 64-entry PC[7:2] bimodal direction\npredictor passed formal and cosim and used 4,201,709 CoreMark cycles,\nversus its parent's 4,491,485, but median Fmax fell to 47.603 MHz and\nfitness 113.29 lost to the concurrently improved core. The current\nchampion keeps integer load forwarding separate from multiplier operands\nand reaches fitness 113.92 at 48.843 MHz with 4,287,652 cycles. The tagged\ntarget predictor saved only about 4,200 additional cycles over direct\ndirection prediction while consuming 96 LUTRAMs, so it is unattractive.\n\nA local experiment built the unchanged canonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration\nCoreMark ELF and recorded its architectural trace with the existing ISS.\nA cycle replay using the contract's xorshift bus-ready draws, selective\nload-to-MUL interlock and divider latency exactly reproduced both parent\ncycle counts above and the earlier predictor's 4,201,709-cycle result.\nOn the current parent it predicts 3,997,209 cycles for the old bimodal\nindex, versus 3,985,515 for the same 64x2 table indexed by\nPC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Conditional misses fall from 51,854 to 41,562.\nA 256-entry, six-bit-history gshare model instead needs 4,005,636 cycles;\nhistory adds cost without a measured accuracy advantage here. Folding\ntwo adjacent PC slices reduces harmful aliases without adding counters,\ntags or