luding the existing sequential-next-PC behavior on alignment\ntraps. Redirect and flush the younger instruction only when this resolved\nnext PC differs from the prediction, gated by ID/EX.valid and EX acceptance\n(!stall_ex_mem). A predicted-taken branch that resolves not taken must\nredirect to its own PC+4. Correctly predicted control transfers must not\nflush or redirect. RVFI pc_wdata remains the actual resolved next PC and\nJAL/JALR link writes remain PC+4.\n\nTrain a conditional branch's indexed counter toward its actual outcome\nexactly once when the valid, legal, aligned branch advances from EX into\nEX/MEM. Never train held EX instructions, bubbles, jumps, or trapped\nbranches. Pre\n…[62616 of 65688 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:32dd234df8a13735]…\nd targets still train, whereas taken trapping branches do\nnot. Wrong-path or held instructions never train or recover. Preserve\ndivider and multiplier arithmetic, OF forwarding, load-use bubbles,\nqueue behavior, memory handshakes and held-WB provenance. Keep nret=1,\nevery top-level port, decoder legality, strict retirement order,\nsymbolic progress and every evaluation invariant unchanged.\n\nThis is one predictor-verification hypothesis: prevalidate static\ntargets and factor the recovery predicate around that registered fact.\nThe intended gain is frequency with identical instructions, predictor\ndecisions, recovery latency and benchmark cycles. Six percent is a\nlow-confidence forecast; the unchanged divider and placement can erase\nthe benefit. At unchanged cycles acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax\nabove approximately 73.62 MHz and fitness above 182.92448 iter/sec.\nRequire all scored placement options and the unchanged unit, formal,\ncosim, canonical CRC/UART, memory-bound and marker-bracketed checks.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
