{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d.get('outcome'),'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'MHz',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),d['title'])\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print('MOTIVATION:',h['motivation'])\n   if any('-r'+str(i)+'s' in d['id'] for i in [5,6,7,8,9]): print('PROPOSAL:',h['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 regression fitness 12.59 MHz 5.292 cycles 4201766 Fetch-time bimodal prediction removes taken-branch bubbles\nMOTIVATION: The current core always fetches PC+4 and flushes the younger instruction\non every taken conditional branch or jump, even for stable CoreMark loop\nbranches. IF/ID is combinational, so steering the PC when an instruction\nis accepted into ID/EX can supply its predicted successor without adding\na pipeline stage. A small direction-only predictor avoids a tagged target\narray and should reduce benchmark cycles while leaving the dominant\ncombinational M-extension timing path structurally unchanged. The local\nhistory has only the baseline and no prior predictor attempts or lessons.\n\nhyp-20261004-003-r2s2 regression fitness 75.18 MHz 31.556 cycles 4197517 PC-only tagged target prediction for branches and indirect jumps\nMOTIVATION: The champion is the multicycle-divider core, with fitness 71.55,\n32.136 MHz median Fmax and 4,491,485 bracketed CoreMark cycles. There\nis no LESSONS.md. The previous predictor attempt, hyp-20261004-003-r1s2,\npassed formal and cosim and reduced cycles to 4,201,766, but used the\nold combinational-divider parent: its 5.292 MHz Fmax produced only\n3.88% improvement over that baseline, below the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nThat result supports the cycle-saving opportunity without establishing\nthe timing cost on the present champion. It predicted direct branches\nand JAL from fetched instruction bits and did not predict JALR.\nCoreMark contains recurrent loop branches, calls and indirect control\ntransfers. Learning their destinations in a small tagged target table\ncan avoid the current one-bubble redirect while keeping instruction\ndecoding and immediate addition out of the fetch-PC prediction path.\n\nhyp-20261004-003-r3s2 regression fitness 113.29 MHz 47.603 cycles 4201709 Direction-only prediction with one-bit branch recovery\nMOTIVATION: No LESSONS.md is present. The two prior predictor attempts passed formal\nand cosim and reduced the roughly 4.49M CoreMark cycles to 4\n…[33505 of 36577 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5d45b6d6801a28ed]…\nhampion scores 174.88 iter/sec at 70.381 MHz and 4,024,573 timed\nCoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Tagged target, loop-exit,\nreturn-shadow and history supplements saved few cycles and lost Fmax;\ndelayed training, delayed squash and registered verification polarity\nalso regressed. A fresh unchanged-parent Gowin option-0 run reproduces\n69.480 MHz and the 19.498 ns EX-comparator-to-OF path, including a\nbranch-condition node with fanout 194. Local comparator retiming and\nshared-comparator experiments did not clear the acceptance margin.\n\nCurrent verification incorporates misalign_fault, which itself includes\nthe actual branch condition. For an unaligned direct target the effective\ntaken direction is always zero, whether the branch is untaken or taken\nand trapping. Direct-target alignment depends only on PC and immediate,\nboth known before EX. Carrying that fact across OF/EX allows verification\nto qualify the prediction directly without rebuilding alignment through\nthe late operand-dependent trap predicate.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
