{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/LESSONS.md'); print('LESSONS:',p.read_text() if p.exists() else '(absent)')\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('id','title','category','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','lutram')})\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d['hypothesis']; print('MOTIVATION:',h.get('motivation') if isinstance(h,dict) else d.get('motivation')); print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "LESSONS: (absent)\n{'id': 'baseline-bench-0e2f75b', 'title': 'Baseline retest for cores/bench', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 12.12, 'fmax_mhz': 5.442, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 12312, 'ff': 460, 'lutram': 8}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-001-r1s0', 'title': 'Share a radix-4 combinational divider across RV32M divide and remainder', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 13.32, 'fmax_mhz': 5.984, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 6154, 'ff': 460, 'lutram': 8}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-002-r1s1', 'title': 'Replace combinational division with a bounded multicycle EX divider', 'category': 'structural', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 71.55, 'fmax_mhz': 32.136, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 2968, 'ff': 642, 'lutram': 8}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-003-r1s2', 'title': 'Fetch-time bimodal prediction removes taken-branch bubbles', 'category': 'predictor', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 12.59, 'fmax_mhz': 5.292, 'cycles': 4201766, 'lut4': 13072, 'ff': 620, 'lutram': 8}\nMOTIVATION: The current core always fetches PC+4 and flushes the younger instruction\non every taken conditional branch or jump, even for stable CoreMark loop\nbranches. IF/ID is combinational, so steering the PC when an instruction\nis accepted into ID/EX can supply its predicted successor without adding\na pipeline stage. A small direction-only predictor avoids a tagged target\narray and should reduce benchmark cycles while leaving the dominant\ncombinational M-extension timing path structurally unchanged. The local\nhistory has only the baseline and no prior predictor attempts or lessons.\n\nNOTES: \n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-001-r2s0', 'title': 'Shorten the registered divider with four parallel-trial radix-4 steps', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 94.28, 'fmax_mhz': 42.346, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 3297, 'ff': 675, 'lutram': 8}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-002-r2s1', 'title': 'Fold the divider to five bit steps per clock with a fused final partial group', 'category': 'structural', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitne\n…[8762 of 11834 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8921b4d47826bb68]…\naches fitness 113.92 at 48.843 MHz with 4,287,652 cycles. The tagged\ntarget predictor saved only about 4,200 additional cycles over direct\ndirection prediction while consuming 96 LUTRAMs, so it is unattractive.\n\nA local experiment built the unchanged canonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration\nCoreMark ELF and recorded its architectural trace with the existing ISS.\nA cycle replay using the contract's xorshift bus-ready draws, selective\nload-to-MUL interlock and divider latency exactly reproduced both parent\ncycle counts above and the earlier predictor's 4,201,709-cycle result.\nOn the current parent it predicts 3,997,209 cycles for the old bimodal\nindex, versus 3,985,515 for the same 64x2 table indexed by\nPC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Conditional misses fall from 51,854 to 41,562.\nA 256-entry, six-bit-history gshare model instead needs 4,005,636 cycles;\nhistory adds cost without a measured accuracy advantage here. Folding\ntwo adjacent PC slices reduces harmful aliases without adding counters,\ntags or speculative state.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
