{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r5s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-003-r5s2\ntitle: \\\"Pipeline predictor training to isolate branch-comparator fanout\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  LESSONS.md is absent. The current champion's folded-PC bimodal predictor\n  achieves fitness 126.34 at 50.352 MHz and 3,985,515 bracketed CoreMark\n  cycles. The previous tagged target predictor used 96 LUTRAMs for little\n  extra cycle reduction, and the prior gshare replay was less accurate.\n  Direction accuracy alone has limited remaining throughput headroom.\n\n  The current IF table updates 128 counter flops directly from accepted\n  EX branch signals. The actual outcome originates in a comparator whose\n  operands can include live MEM load data. This same comparator also\n  drives immediate misprediction recovery. Local Boolean counter updates\n  removed an indexed training-read mux in an earlier implementation and\n  improved median Fmax from 47.199 to 47.603 MHz, supporting investigation\n  of the training circuit rather than adding predictor capacity. Register\n  the accepted training event before it drives the counter bank, reducing\n  the comparator's load and separating recovery from counter update logic.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one clock of latency to direction-table training, while retaining\n  the existing 64-entry two-bit folded-PC bimodal predictor and immediate\n  EX verification. In if_stage, capture the accepted EX training event\n  into eight bits of state: valid, six-bit index and actual taken outcome.\n  Drive all per-entry decoded write enables and local Boolean saturation\n  equations exclusively from these registered training signals. Do not\n  register branch verification or PC recovery, and do not change the\n  instruction pipeline, memory organization, arithmetic, predictor table\n  size, hash, initialization, direction encoding or retirement width.\n\n  The training register accepts at most one event each clock, matching\n  nret=1 and the existing single EX acceptance boundary. The previous\n  registered event updates its counter on that same edge, so back-to-back\n  accepted branches sustain one update per clock without a queue or loss.\n  Capture only the event's index and outcome, never a snapshot of counter\n  state: apply each event to the current counter state in original branch\n  acceptance order. Consecutive same-index events must therefore compose\n  correctly even when their outcomes alternate or a counter saturates.\n\n  Use exactly the current PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8] index and weakly taken (10)\n  counter reset state. Table lookup reads old registered counter MSBs.\n  Do not bypass either the current EX outcome or the pending registered\n  update into fetch. A branch may consequently make a guess from one\n  additional clock of old history; carry the actual chosen predicted_taken\n  bit with its accepted instruction, as today, so recovery remains exact.\n  A simultaneous table update and lookup must obey old-state edge behavior.\n\n  Capture valid independently of frontend and backend holding: the input\n  train_valid already qualifies an accepted legal nontrapping EX branch.\n  Every clock consumes the old pending event and captures the new valid\n  bit, including capturing zero on bubbles or stalled EX cycles. Thus a\n  later imem stall, dmem stall, division wait or load-to-MUL hold neither\n  blocks an older accepted update nor repeats it. A younger redirect must\n  not discard an older accepted pending event. Reset clears pending valid\n  and resets all counters with priority over training; pre-reset events\n  must never leak into the post-reset table. Index/outcome payload need\n  not reset because an invalid pending event cannot enable any write.\n\n  Keep the current fetch-time legal BRANCH/JAL predecode and aligned exact\n  direct targets, immediate conditional effective_taken XOR predicted_taken\n  recovery, registered-prediction selection of the recovery address, and\n  unresolved JALR behavior. EX still verifies branches and emits training\n  once on acceptance. Preserve all prediction capture/hold/flush behavior,\n  actual RVFI next PCs and links, legality and alignment traps, wrong-path\n  side-effect suppression, nret=1 IO, strict retirement order, all bus\n  handshakes and held-WB forwarding provenance. No target table, return\n  stack, speculative history, caches or divider changes are included.\n\n  The expected improvement comes from isolating high-fanout counter-bank\n  controls from the live-load branch comparator, at the cost of eight\n  flops and slightly delayed learning. Keep the implementation explicitly\n  clocked so synthesis cannot reintroduce an EX-to-table training bypass.\n  Confidence is low: the current reported worst path is inside the\n  unchanged divider, so predictor isolation may not raise median Fmax\n  enough. Acceptance requires fitness above 132.15164, measured using\n  median Gowin timing and canonical marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles.\n  Preserve all existing unit, formal, cosim, CRC, UART and bounds gates.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Insert an eight-bit one-event training register between accepted EX\n      events and the existing 64 local two-bit counter update blocks.\n      Update counters from the previous registered valid/index/outcome;\n      capture new event validity every clock, independent of fetch holds.\n      Preserve counter reset, hash, lookup, raw predecode, predicted target\n      generation and PC priorities. Reset pending valid and never bypass\n      pending/current training into prediction.\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: |\n      Extend the independent table model with one pending event. Retain\n      all saturation, isolation, raw-predecode, alignment, old-state lookup\n      and PC-priority assertions, accounting for the explicit training\n      latency. Check back-to-back same-index and different-index updates,\n      alternating directions, saturation crossings, bubbles that drain a\n      pending event, frontend holds during application, and reset with a\n      pending update. Confirm invalid pending payload cannot touch a table\n      entry and no combinational current/pending-event bypass exists.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Track accepted EX branch events and their one-clock-delayed table\n      applications independently. Under directed and randomized imem/dmem\n      holds, verify every surviving event applies once and in order, while\n      held EX branches do not create duplicate events. Include consecutive\n      branches hashing to the same entry, recovery concurrent with an older\n      pending event, load-dependent branches, divider waits and reset.\n      Retain exact RVFI PC/order/operand/link/trap checks, immediate recovery\n      scheduling and wrong-path memory/divide suppression. Canonical\n      CoreMark CRCs and marker-bracketed cycles remain mandatory.\n''')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()); jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261004-003-r5s2' and d['category']=='predictor'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Refined hypothesis is schema-valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
