{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/LESSONS.md')\nprint('LESSONS:', p.read_text() if p.exists() else 'absent')\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(l)\n print({k:r.get(k) for k in ['id','title','category','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff']})\n if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('MOTIVATION:', h.get('motivation',r.get('motivation')) if isinstance(h,dict) else '')\n  print('PROPOSAL:',h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "LESSONS: absent\n{'id': 'baseline-bench-0e2f75b', 'title': 'Baseline retest for cores/bench', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 12.12, 'fmax_mhz': 5.442, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 12312, 'ff': 460}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-001-r1s0', 'title': 'Share a radix-4 combinational divider across RV32M divide and remainder', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 13.32, 'fmax_mhz': 5.984, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 6154, 'ff': 460}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-002-r1s1', 'title': 'Replace combinational division with a bounded multicycle EX divider', 'category': 'structural', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 71.55, 'fmax_mhz': 32.136, 'cycles': 4491485, 'lut4': 2968, 'ff': 642}\n{'id': 'hyp-20261004-003-r1s2', 'title': 'Fetch-time bimodal prediction removes taken-branch bubbles', 'category': 'predictor', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 12.59, 'fmax_mhz': 5.292, 'cycles': 4201766, 'lut4': 13072, 'ff': 620}\nMOTIVATION: The current core always fetches PC+4 and flushes the younger instruction\non every taken conditional branch or jump, even for stable CoreMark loop\nbranches. IF/ID is combinational, so steering the PC when an instruction\nis accepted into ID/EX can supply its predicted successor without adding\na pipeline stage. A small direction-only predictor avoids a tagged target\narray and should reduce benchmark cycles while leaving the dominant\ncombinational M-extension timing path structurally unchanged. The local\nhistory has only the baseline and no prior predictor attempts or lessons.\n\nPROPOSAL: Add one compact fetch-time control-flow predictor: a 64-entry bimodal\nbranch-history table of two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[7:2]\nand reset to weakly not taken (01). Predecode only legal RV32I conditional\nbranches and JAL from the raw fetched instruction. For a conditional\nbranch, use the counter MSB as the direction; predict JAL taken without\nconsulting the table. Form direct targets from PC plus the B/J immediate,\nand predict only word-aligned targets. All other instructio\n…[29677 of 32749 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a03dc8b788b8acac]…\n verifies branches and emits training\nonce on acceptance. Preserve all prediction capture/hold/flush behavior,\nactual RVFI next PCs and links, legality and alignment traps, wrong-path\nside-effect suppression, nret=1 IO, strict retirement order, all bus\nhandshakes and held-WB forwarding provenance. No target table, return\nstack, speculative history, caches or divider changes are included.\n\nThe expected improvement comes from isolating high-fanout counter-bank\ncontrols from the live-load branch comparator, at the cost of eight\nflops and slightly delayed learning. Keep the implementation explicitly\nclocked so synthesis cannot reintroduce an EX-to-table training bypass.\nConfidence is low: the current reported worst path is inside the\nunchanged divider, so predictor isolation may not raise median Fmax\nenough. Acceptance requires fitness above 132.15164, measured using\nmedian Gowin timing and canonical marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles.\nPreserve all existing unit, formal, cosim, CRC, UART and bounds gates.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
