{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python - <<'PY'\nimport json, yaml\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r6s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-003-r6s2',\n'title':'Registered predictor recovery with same-cycle target refetch',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''LESSONS.md is absent. The champion already has folded-PC bimodal\nprediction and a two-entry fetch queue. Broad tagged target prediction\npreviously saved little throughput and used 96 LUTRAMs; delayed training\nalone regressed. A local canonical CoreMark trace contains 529,588 timed\nconditional branches but only 13,735 returns. The current folded table\nhas 41,562 EX-order direction misses, so extra predictor accuracy or a\nreturn stack has too little independent cycle-saving headroom.\n\nThe champion's Gowin option-0 report reproduces 51.166 MHz and shows\nlive dmem-to-fetch-queue paths at 24.837 ns, only 0.001 ns below the\n24.838 ns divider path. Branch recovery currently feeds queue write\ncontrols and decode acceptance directly from the live load comparator.\nRegistering predictor recovery can remove this long feedback cone;\nimmediately fetching through the registered target avoids an added\nmisprediction bubble and preserves the tight divider liveness window.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Retain the current folded-PC 64x2 direction predictor and exact aligned\nBRANCH/JAL predecode, but register the accepted EX recovery event and\nits preselected full target before they control fetch and hazards.\nCapture a valid event only when the resolving instruction actually\nadvances from EX, with the existing reset, legal, alignment and older\nmemory-stall qualifications. Capture the same recovery address as today:\nconditional PC+4 versus direct target is selected from the instruction's\nregistered predicted_taken bit; JALR uses its resolved target. Reset\nclears event validity; every subsequent clock captures new validity,\nincluding zero for bubbles, so a recovery cannot replay.\n\nCrucially, use the registered event as an immediate recovery-cycle\nfetch-address bypass, not just a delayed synchronous PC assignment.\nDuring that cycle drive io_imemAddr from its registered recovery target,\nignore all old queue entries, and offer the fresh target instruction\nand its prediction directly to ID if io_imemReady is high. The younger\ninstruction currently in EX is killed on this same edge. If ID accepts\nthe recovery word, install its predicted successor as the next fetch\nPC and leave the queue empty. If the recovery word is available but\ncannot be decoded, retain it as the sole new queue head and advance\nPC once. If the bus is stalled, leave the queue empty and install the\nrecovery target as the retry PC. Reset overrides all operations.\n\nWith zero-wait recovery, the target instruction enters ID/EX on the\nsame clock as in the current combinational-recovery design: the old\nimplementation redirects PC at the branch's EX edge, while the new\nimplementation exposes the registered target throughout the following\ncycle and accepts it at that cycle's edge. Do not introduce an extra\nbranch, JALR or branch-to-DIV bubble. The queue's wrong-path words are\nignored during recovery and cannot be accepted, counted or retained.\nCapture PC, raw instruction and predicted_taken atomically from the\nrecovery target; its own direct prediction can steer the following PC.\nDo not mask instruction bits with a late live EX comparator.\n\nChange recovery arbitration so the fresh target word is permitted into\nID/EX instead of flushing that word. Explicitly kill the existing\nwrong-path EX instruction: suppress EX/MEM valid, divider start/wait,\nits branch training and further recovery generation. A wrong-path load\nin ID/EX must not impose a load-to-MUL hazard on the target instruction.\nThe older resolving branch or jump already in EX/MEM continues normally,\nincluding its link result, actual PC and trap metadata. Preserve older\nMEM/WB writes and held-WB forwarding. No younger instruction may issue\na memory request, write a register, start a divide, train the table or\nretire after recovery. A recovery event is captured only after older\nMEM backpressure clears, so a surviving request is never discarded.\n\nKeep accepted branch training at its existing EX boundary, with the\nsame per-entry Boolean counter updates, folded index, weakly-taken\nreset and old-state lookup. Its only new qualification is suppression\nof the EX instruction killed by an older registered recovery. Preserve\nall actual RVFI next PCs, link values, target alignment traps, decoder\nlegality, nret=1 IO, +1 retirement order, symbolic liveness and memory\nhandshakes. This hypothesis changes the predictor recovery circuit,\nwithout changing arithmetic, memory organization, table capacity or\npipeline retirement latency. Require unchanged canonical CRC/UART and\nbounds checks. Acceptance needs fitness above 140.78114, corresponding\nto median Fmax above approximately 53.913 MHz if cycles remain 3,829,487.\nThe unchanged divider and integer datapath still limit frequency, so\nthe gain is uncertain and must be measured across all Gowin placements.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Register accepted recovery valid and its existing preselected target.\nOn an older pending recovery, kill the younger EX payload, prevent\nnew recovery/training, and suppress wrong-path divider launch and\nwait. Preserve the resolving instruction's EX/MEM transfer and RVFI,\nall arithmetic, actual next PCs, traps and forwarding.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Bypass the fetch address with the registered recovery target and\nignore the old queue head during recovery. Offer the fresh target\nword through empty bypass, clearing old occupancy and retaining\nonly an accepted but unconsumed recovery word. On bus stall install\nthe retry target; on acceptance install that word's predicted next\nPC. Preserve the bimodal table, queue ordering and raw predecode.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Allow the recovery-target instruction into ID rather than flushing\nit. Suppress load-use/divide holds originating from the killed\nwrong-path EX instruction, while preserving true older memory\nbackpressure and ordinary selective load-to-MUL arbitration.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire registered recovery consistently to fetch, decode arbitration\nand younger-EX kill semantics. Preserve all nret=1 ports, retirement\nlogic and the independent held-WB forwarding provenance.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_fetch_queue.py','description':'''Check recovery target bypass at empty, one-entry and full occupancy,\nwith independent ready/accept signals. Verify discard of every old\nentry, capture of only the new target word, no duplicates, target\nprediction capture, retry on imem stall and reset priority.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_predictor.py','description':'''Retain hash, saturation, raw legality, alignment and old-state tests.\nCheck prediction of the recovery target's own BRANCH/JAL and capture\nof exactly the prediction used for the next accepted fetch.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Verify both conditional mismatch directions and JALR recovery with\nunchanged target acceptance timing. Exercise queued wrong-path\nstores, loads, MUL, DIV and branches; ensure no side effects or\ntraining survive. Cover imem/dmem stalls, trap cases and reset,\ncomplete RVFI/order/operand/link checks, and branch/JALR-to-DIV\nliveness schedules without weakening any existing assertions.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=80))\nimport jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Refined hypothesis is schema-valid.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
