op hits 5373 overrides 607\n4 RR misses 31276 loop hits 148146 overrides 10524\n4 LRU misses 31276 loop hits 148146 overrides 10524\n4 low_conf misses 38805 loop hits 67536 overrides 2761\n8 RR misses 31278 loop hits 148214 overrides 10546\n8 LRU misses 31278 loop hits 148214 overrides 10546\n8 low_conf misses 34429 loop hits 106068 overrides 7139\n16 RR misses 31256 loop hits 149029 overrides 10578\n16 LRU misses 31256 loop hits 149029 overrides 10578\n16 low_conf misses 30056 loop hits 159430 overrides 11782\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r7s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-003-r7s2',\n'title':'Four-entry loop-exit predictor supplements folded-PC direction prediction',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''LESSONS.md is absent. The champion already has a 64x2 folded-PC bimodal predictor and a two-entry fetch queue. Prior broad tagged target prediction used substantial storage for little additional cycle benefit. Delayed training and delayed squash both regressed, with the unchanged divider remaining critical. Another recovery retiming attempt has weak support.\n\nA local architectural trace of the canonical 2K, ten-iteration program contains 529,588 timed conditional branches, 13,735 JALRs and no timed division operations. An EX-order-equivalent sequential direction model has 41,566 misses for the current folded bimodal predictor. Agreement encoding barely improves this, to 41,175, and small gshare tables worsen it. A generic four-entry fully associative loop-count supplement reduces modeled misses to 31,276, nearly matching an eight-entry version's 31,278. Stable loop exits are therefore the strongest small predictor opportunity found, without a large local-history pattern table.\n\nThese are architectural trace-model results from an existing simulator build, not measurements of a candidate implementation. They exclude speculative fetch lookahead, queue timing and placement effects. The modeled reduction is about 25 percent of direction misses but only a small fraction of total cycles; it does not establish a gain above the 4.6 percent acceptance margin.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one compact loop-exit predictor alongside the existing folded-PC bimodal fallback. Use exactly four fully associative entries keyed by the full architectural branch PC. Each entry has valid, a 32-bit PC tag, an 8-bit running taken count, an 8-bit learned taken-trip count, a 2-bit confidence counter and an overflow flag. There is no target cache: the current fetched instruction continues to supply the exact B/J immediate and alignment check. Use four parallel valid-qualified tag comparisons and one-hot selection, rather than a serial priority chain in the fetch-PC path. Approximately 208 state bits, including replacement control, are sufficient.\n\nAt fetch, a legal word-aligned backward conditional branch may override the bimodal direction only on a valid tag hit with confidence at least two, a nonzero learned trip count and no overflow. Predict not taken precisely when the running taken count equals the learned trip count; otherwise predict taken. Every miss, low-confidence entry, forward branch, overflowed entry and ineligible encoding uses the existing predictor. JAL remains directly predicted and JALR remains resolved in EX. Continue to train the original 64x2 table toward actual direction for every accepted eligible conditional branch, preserving its index, weakly-taken reset and local Boolean saturation logic.\n\nTrain loop state only at the existing accepted, legal, nontrapping EX conditional-branch boundary, using the resolving instruction's registered PC, immediate sign and fully forwarded actual condition. A backward-branch tag miss allocates the first invalid entry, or the round-robin victim when all four entries are valid. Advance the two-bit replacement pointer only on an allocation that evicts a valid entry; do not pin confident entries. Initialize a new entry's running count to one for taken or zero for not taken, learned trip count and confidence to zero, and overflow to zero.\n\nFor a matching accepted taken branch, increment its running count. At 255, set overflow and clear confidence instead of wrapping; keep overflow set until a not-taken outcome. On a matching not-taken branch, an unoverflowed nonzero running count that equals the learned trip count increments confidence with saturation at three. Otherwise install the observed count as the learned trip count and clear confidence; after overflow install zero. Then clear running count and overflow. This learns an exit only after repeated equal completed trips, adapts to variable bounds and handles loops longer than the counter range safely. Never truncate PC tags or seed entries with benchmark addresses, counts or results.\n\nUse committed EX updates only: do not add speculative iteration counters, rollback checkpoints or a training bypass. Fetch and training on the same edge observe old loop state. In a very short loop or under deep fetch lookahead, this can use a stale iteration count; that is an ordinary direction prediction error and must recover through the existing exact EX check. Capture the actual chosen predicted_taken direction in each accepted bypassed or queued word, retain it through holds and never recompute a held word's prediction from newer loop state. Reset clears valid bits and replacement control; uninitialized payloads must be completely masked by invalid tags. Reset wins over allocation and training.\n\nPreserve immediate effective_taken XOR predicted_taken correction, registered-prediction selection of the recovery address, wrong-path queue discard, actual RVFI next PC and JAL/JALR link values. Retain all current decode legality, target traps, divider and memory acceptance gates, held-WB forwarding, single-channel nret=1 IO, retirement order and symbolic progress. Counts are advisory and may never suppress architectural branch verification or produce architectural results. Do not add a BTB, return stack, global history, cache, arithmetic change or new pipeline stage.\n\nValidate exact state transitions, confidence gating, both mismatch directions, instruction changes at a learned PC, short-loop stale-count recovery, loop-bound changes, count overflow and all queue occupancies under independent instruction/data stalls. Run the unchanged correctness gates and canonical CRC/UART/bounds checks. Score the implementation with marker-bracketed cycles and median Gowin Fmax across all placement options. The forecast is deliberately low: approximately ten thousand fewer modeled misses may save roughly thirteen thousand cycles, around 0.35 percent of the champion's 3,829,487 cycles. Hardware timing and lookahead can erase this. Clearing fitness 140.78114 would still require a material Fmax gain, so do not describe the trace result as an accepted fitness improvement.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':1,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add the four-entry tagged loop-count table, round-robin replacement and accepted-EX training inputs. Implement confidence-qualified backward-branch direction override with parallel valid-gated tag lookup, bounded counts, overflow handling and local clocked updates. Preserve the existing folded-PC bimodal table, raw direct-control legality/alignment, old-state lookup, actual-direction metadata capture, queue and recovery priorities.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Export the accepted resolving branch's full PC and its own B-immediate sign alongside the existing actual-direction training signal and folded index. Keep the train_valid qualification, forwarded comparator, direction verification, redirect target, traps, links and arithmetic unchanged.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire full training PC and backward-branch qualification from EX to IF as internal signals. Keep existing train_valid/index/taken wiring, immediate recovery and all top-level memory and nret=1 RVFI ports unchanged.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_predictor.py','description':'''Add an independent loop-table model and exact state-transition checks for allocation, full-tag hits/misses, round-robin eviction, repeated-trip confidence, variable bounds, zero trips, 255/256 boundaries, overflow retention, reset and simultaneous old-state lookup/update. Retain current bimodal saturation, raw legality, alignment and JAL assertions.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_fetch_queue.py','description':'''Model the combined direction choice and verify that queued/bypassed words retain the prediction actually used at their acceptance edge, even when training, eviction or overflow later changes the table. Preserve occupancy, bus-ready, decode-accept, reset and redirect assertions.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Exercise constant and changing loop bounds, nested/interleaved loops, more than four active PCs, full-tag aliases, short loops with fetch lookahead, source dependencies, traps, changed instructions at a learned PC and independent bus stalls. Verify accepted training provenance, both recovery directions, absence of wrong-path side effects, exact RVFI operands/targets/links/order and unchanged liveness requirements.'''},\n]\n}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper):\n def represent_str(self,data):\n  return self.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data or len(data)>180 else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,Dumper.represent_str)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(h,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\nprint('Wrote',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
