{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_48", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r14s0.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r14s0',\n 'title':'Remove duplicated WB write-first muxes from core register-file reads',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 174.88 iter/sec at 70.381 MHz with 4,024,573 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Its separate operand stage already resolves youngest EX, then MEM, then retained WB, then RF at the OF/EX edge. Nevertheless each RF read also compares the same source address against the current WB write and muxes in WB data, duplicating address comparison and a 32-bit data mux before the explicit forwarding selection. Prior forwarding-match predecode and one-hot selector rewrites regressed; this proposal leaves that selector unchanged and instead removes a logically redundant source-path mux. Several arithmetic/control scratch trials also regressed, reinforcing the value of eliminating duplicated logic without changing producer scheduling.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Add a compile-time WRITE_FIRST_BYPASS parameter to reg_file.sv, default true so standalone instances retain their existing documented write-first interface. Gate only the two same-cycle w_en/w_addr read bypass arms by that constant. Instantiate reg_file in core.sv with WRITE_FIRST_BYPASS=false. Each core RF read then returns zero for x0 and the stored architectural register value otherwise, with no WB data selection at the RF output. Preserve the synchronous write, reset initialization, register array and write-address/x0 filtering exactly. Do not replace the array, remove reset, infer new memories or add a registered read port.\n\nThe existing operand_stage/forward_unit selection remains the authority for same-cycle WB values. For every valid legal current WB write, wb_fwd_valid_q and the MEM/WB reg_write/legal fields already authorize the identical WB source. If a younger accepted EX writer or MEM writer matches, it retains priority; otherwise a matching WB selects wb_w_data directly before OF capture. Therefore a core instruction that previously selected RF's internal WB bypass must now select the identical explicit WB path. When no WB match exists, the raw RF value is unchanged. Held WB provenance during dmem stalls stays exact. After the synchronous write the stored RF value is current regardless of the explicit forwarding source's later validity. Check this equivalence under reset, retirement, stalled WB payload retention and overlapping EX/MEM/WB writers, rather than assuming WB valid always remains asserted.\n\nKeep every OF selection equation, validity/payload enable, source-x0 override, use_rs1/use_rs2 load interlock, verification-only unused-source correction and accepted producer gate unchanged. Preserve the six-stage pipeline, combinational multiply, six-RUN real/ALTOPS divider, prediction/recovery/training, memory handshakes and alignment traps, decoder default-illegal and EBREAK-only SYSTEM rules, nret=1 IO, exact RVFI source/results/order and symbolic progress. No instruction latency, stage, interlock, result schedule or evaluation/correctness relaxation is added. This is one local removal of duplicate write-first bypass logic in the core instance.\n\nThe intended gain is shorter raw RF-to-OF routing, fewer duplicated WB address comparisons and sixty-four fewer wide bypass bits, with identical CoreMark cycles. Six percent is a low-confidence forecast: the multiplier/divider/control paths may remain dominant and placement can erase the benefit. Acceptance at identical cycles requires median vendor Fmax above approximately 73.62 MHz and fitness above 182.92448 iter/sec. Require both RF parameter modes to pass their documented semantics, unchanged pipeline/formal/stalled RVFI cosim gates, canonical five CRCs and UART literal, memory bounds and marker-bracketed timing.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'Add default-true WRITE_FIRST_BYPASS parameter and use it only as a constant guard on both existing same-cycle WB read bypass arms. With false, reads expose x0 or stored register values; retain all reset, synchronous write, x0 and array behavior.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Instantiate reg_file with WRITE_FIRST_BYPASS=false while preserving the existing explicit EX/MEM/WB-to-OF forwarding network and retained-WB provenance unchanged. Preserve all top-level ports and pipeline connections.'},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'Retain default write-first tests and add a false-parameter elaboration verifying raw pre-edge stored reads, post-edge synchronous writes, x0 and reset behavior. Parameter-mode tests must assert the distinct documented semantics rather than weaken current assertions.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add same-edge WB-to-OF operand cases with matching rs1/rs2, x0, both-source matches, overlapping younger EX/MEM writers, held MEM/WB payload during random/directed dmem stalls, reset and redirects. Independently verify exact RVFI source/result/order fields and preserve existing load/divide and side-effect assertions.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=100))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated duplicate RF bypass hypothesis')\nPY\ncat /srv/hwebench/tmp/e4e97874/b737a704/rfbypass-tests.log; tail -5 cores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p0/gw.log\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
