{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r14s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261005-002-r14s1\ntitle: \\\"Committed store queue decouples pipeline advance from RAM write acceptance\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 174.88 iter/sec at 70.381 MHz with 4,024,573\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Structural\n  history improved operand registration and divider scheduling, but\n  multiplier pipelining, early loads and spatial divider stages regressed.\n  No prior structural attempt introduced a committed store queue. The\n  current hazard unit freezes EX/MEM and every younger stage whenever\n  any MEM load or store sees io_dmemReady=0. Ordinary RAM stores need no\n  returned value, yet approximately 22 percent bus backpressure stops\n  arithmetic, operand capture and decode. A small queue can separate\n  architectural store commitment from physical bus service.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a two-entry FIFO of committed ordinary-RAM stores behind MEM.\n  An aligned legal store to [0x00000000,0x00100000) completes its MEM\n  pipeline transfer when either the external bus accepts it directly or\n  the FIFO accepts its address, replicated write data and four-bit mask.\n  Its normal MEM/WB payload then retires exactly once on channel 0,\n  even if io_dmemReady was low at enqueue. The FIFO owns the irrevocable\n  write until bus acknowledgment. This is one structural change to MEM\n  completion and pipeline backpressure. Keep the six stages and nret=1.\n\n  For an empty queue retain direct-store fall-through: drive the current\n  MEM store on the bus and, if ready, advance without saving a duplicate.\n  If not ready, enqueue the same request and advance the pipeline. Its\n  address/data/mask must remain identical as ownership moves from EX/MEM\n  to the queue. With queued entries, the oldest alone owns the write bus\n  and pops only on io_dmemReady. A younger RAM store may enqueue on that\n  same edge; a full queue accepts a replacement when its head dequeues.\n  Preserve FIFO order and every store; do not merge stores. Drain queued\n  requests during normal execution, divider waits, imem stalls and\n  front-end holds. Redirects never discard committed entries.\n\n  Loads remain blocking and issue only when the store FIFO is empty.\n  Do not add alias comparison or store-to-load forwarding. After an older\n  queued store drains on an edge, a held load may begin the next cycle.\n  Every accepted load therefore includes every older architectural store,\n  including overlapping byte and halfword writes. Preserve extraction,\n  alignment traps, load-use bubbles and accepted MEM-to-OF forwarding.\n  A load waiting behind stores must not expose the queue head's address\n  or data as its RVFI access or become a premature forwarding producer.\n\n  Every access outside ordinary RAM follows the existing serialized\n  direct path after the FIFO drains. UART and both benchmark marker\n  stores MUST wait for external bus acknowledgment before advancing\n  MEM or retiring. Never enqueue MMIO or alias a high address to RAM.\n  Start is acknowledged after preceding stores; stop is acknowledged\n  after all benchmark stores. The scored timing window must include\n  outstanding benchmark writes. EBREAK and accepted MISC-MEM/FENCE\n  instructions also wait for an empty FIFO, so termination and fences\n  cannot abandon committed writes. Preserve current decoder legality.\n\n  Export actual MEM-blocked from instruction completion rules: ordinary\n  store capacity, load/MMIO queue draining and bus readiness, or a\n  fence/EBREAK drain barrier. Replace the blanket memory-op&&\"'!ready\n  freeze with this signal. A pending FIFO write alone does not hold a\n  nonmemory instruction. When MEM really blocks, preserve EX/MEM and\n  younger-stage holds, MEM/WB valid clearing and retained-WB forwarding\n  provenance. Otherwise MEM/WB captures the current pipeline instruction\n  once, never the bus owner'\"'s previously committed instruction. Divider\n  launch/consume and accepted redirects respect actual older MEM blocking.\n\n  Separate architectural MEM payload from physical bus ownership. At\n  enqueue, RVFI carries that store's original PC, sources, aligned\n  address, mask and replicated data through the existing retirement\n  boundary. Dequeue produces no retirement and never increments order.\n  At a direct load, RVFI captures its accepted read word. Physical dmem\n  outputs select the oldest queued store or the direct request; read and\n  write ownership are mutually exclusive. Hold requests stable while\n  io_dmemReady=0. Resolve coincident direct acceptance, enqueue and dequeue\n  explicitly to prevent duplicate writes. Only valid, legal, aligned\n  current MEM stores enqueue; wrong-path or trapping stores never do.\n\n  Reset clears FIFO occupancy and cancels requests as it resets the\n  existing pipeline. Preserve exact +1 retirement order and source values,\n  real/ALTOPS arithmetic and availability, decoder default-illegal and\n  EBREAK-only SYSTEM rules, memory bounds, branch recovery and unchanged\n  symbolic liveness checks. Under ready-bus symbolic streams sustain\n  one store per clock through direct service or simultaneous pop/enqueue;\n  draining must not add an unbounded phase. Change no contract file.\n\n  Forecast eight percent greater fitness with low confidence. Fewer\n  global store-stall cycles must compensate for FIFO and arbitration\n  timing costs; loads after stores still serialize. The final store-queue\n  circuit is unimplemented and unmeasured. Alternative divider-lane and\n  forwarding-topology scratch probes did not clear acceptance and are\n  excluded; no measured gain is claimed for this proposal. Require\n  actual median Gowin Fmax times canonical bracketed iterations/cycle\n  above 182.92448 iter/sec. At unchanged Fmax that requires more than\n  4.4 percent fewer cycles; with six percent fewer cycles the required\n  Fmax is approximately 69.20 MHz. Validate every unchanged formal/cosim\n  gate, canonical CRCs, UART validation literal, memory bounds and marker\n  drain ordering before judging the scored placements.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/store_queue.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a two-entry FIFO of committed full-address stores, replicated\n      data and byte masks. Implement stable oldest-head bus service,\n      acknowledge-only dequeue, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue and full\n      replacement, occupancy reset and explicit capacity. Redirects\n      never invalidate committed entries.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Separate instruction completion/RVFI capture from bus ownership.\n      Integrate direct-store fall-through, irrevocable enqueue and\n      autonomous draining, conservative load ordering, direct MMIO\n      acknowledgment and FENCE/EBREAK drain barriers. Export actual\n      blocking; preserve alignment traps, load extraction, one-time\n      MEM/WB capture and payload retention during holds.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Use actual MEM blocking instead of blanket bus-not-ready blocking.\n      Preserve load-use and divider arbitration, redirect priority,\n      payload holds, validity kills and older-stage draining. A queued\n      write alone must not stall an independent ALU instruction.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire actual MEM blocking while preserving external IO and nret=1\n      retirement. Retain forwarding age and held-WB provenance. Enable\n      load forwarding only from the current accepted direct load, never\n      from the physical queued-write owner.\n  - file: test/test_store_queue.py\n    description: |\n      Check FIFO and accepted-write traces against an independent model\n      through empty/full transitions, simultaneous pop/enqueue, long\n      ready-low bursts, changing rejected inputs and reset. Assert stable\n      outstanding requests and no lost, duplicate or reordered writes.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise RAM-store bursts with independent arithmetic, dependent\n      loads and branches under directed and random bus stalls. Verify\n      one retirement at enqueue, one eventual accepted write, byte and\n      halfword overlap, pressure, wrong-path suppression, load values,\n      held-WB forwarding and exact RVFI order. Check drain-before-ack\n      UART/start/stop stores, FENCE and EBREAK, including stop immediately\n      after a final stalled benchmark store. Keep all correctness and\n      progress assertions; adapt directed store timing expectations only\n      to the specified enqueue-versus-bus-ack completion semantics.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport jsonschema,yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r14s1.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261005-002-r14s1' and h['category']=='structural'\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert -50<=h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']<=50\nprint('Schema-valid final hypothesis:',p)\nprint('Acceptance MHz with 6% fewer cycles:',70.381*1.046*.94)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
