{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')\nfor line in p.read_text().splitlines():\n e=json.loads(line)\n if e.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(e['id'], e.get('outcome'), 'fitness=',e.get('fitness'), 'fmax=',e.get('fmax_mhz'), 'cycles=',e.get('cycles'))\n  print(e['title'])\n  h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print((h.get('motivation','') if isinstance(h,dict) else e.get('motivation',''))[:1600])\n  print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else str(h))[:1600])\n  print()\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 regression fitness= 12.59 fmax= 5.292 cycles= 4201766\nFetch-time bimodal prediction removes taken-branch bubbles\nThe current core always fetches PC+4 and flushes the younger instruction\non every taken conditional branch or jump, even for stable CoreMark loop\nbranches. IF/ID is combinational, so steering the PC when an instruction\nis accepted into ID/EX can supply its predicted successor without adding\na pipeline stage. A small direction-only predictor avoids a tagged target\narray and should reduce benchmark cycles while leaving the dominant\ncombinational M-extension timing path structurally unchanged. The local\nhistory has only the baseline and no prior predictor attempts or lessons.\n\nAdd one compact fetch-time control-flow predictor: a 64-entry bimodal\nbranch-history table of two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[7:2]\nand reset to weakly not taken (01). Predecode only legal RV32I conditional\nbranches and JAL from the raw fetched instruction. For a conditional\nbranch, use the counter MSB as the direction; predict JAL taken without\nconsulting the table. Form direct targets from PC plus the B/J immediate,\nand predict only word-aligned targets. All other instructions, including\nJALR, predict PC+4. Keep direction state only: no BTB, global history,\nreturn stack, target cache, or instruction buffering.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF/ID and ID/EX. In EX compute the existing architecturally resolved next\nPC, including the existing sequential-next-PC behavior on alignment\ntraps. Redirect and flush the younger instruction only when this resolved\nnext PC differs from the prediction, gated by ID/EX.valid and EX acceptance\n(!stall_ex_mem). A predicted-taken branch that resolves not taken must\nredirect to its own PC+4. Correctly predicted control transfers must not\nflush or redirect. RVFI pc_wdata remains the actual resolved next PC and\nJAL/JALR link writes remain PC+4.\n\nTrain a conditional branch's indexed counter toward its actual outcome\nexactly once \n…[33685 of 36757 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:637ddc230c0b8df0]…\nd-less or bit 3 for signed-greater-or-equal; BLTU/BGEU select bit 4 for unsigned-less or bit 5 for unsigned-greater-or-equal. Other comparison arms are zero. Bit 6 is !aligned && predicted_taken. Reserved comparison classes select no relation. This seventh bit preserves verification even for inconsistent prediction metadata: an unaligned direct target has effective direction zero.\n\nRegister this mode with its instruction in ID/OF under the existing payload !stall rule. OF only copies the matching mode into OF/EX at its existing accepted operand edge. Reset clears it with each bundle; holds preserve it, and flush kills validity exactly as today. Retain the original prediction bit for choosing the conditional recovery address. No IF/ID or queue metadata, stage, speculative history or deferred squash is added. The final extra state is fourteen bits, seven at each existing register boundary.\n\nIn EX form recovery_wrong as a balanced OR of the six masked ordinary full-width operand relations and bit 6. Consume th\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
