{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Reduce destructive branch aliasing with a larger folded-PC direction table\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion uses only 64 untagged two-bit direction counters. Independent\n  conditional branches that hash to the same entry can repeatedly train it\n  in opposite directions. The earlier folded-PC predictor materially reduced\n  CoreMark cycles, whereas recent recovery retiming attempts retained\n  4,024,573 cycles and lost median Fmax against the 70.381 MHz champion.\n  Predictor accuracy is therefore the promising remaining angle: remove\n  destructive direction aliasing without changing recovery or arithmetic.\n  No LESSONS.md is present; the recorded predictor experiments were reviewed.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the 64-entry bimodal conditional-direction table with a fixed\n  256-entry table of two-bit saturating counters. Index fetch and training\n  identically with PC[9:2] XOR PC[17:10]. Keep weakly-taken reset, local\n  per-entry Boolean saturation, old-state lookup and no training bypass.\n  Only the selected MSB participates in fetch. The wider generic PC hash\n  uses full architectural PC bits without benchmark addresses or assumptions\n  about where particular routines reside. Keep exact raw legal/aligned\n  BRANCH/JAL predecode and direct target generation; JALR remains resolved\n  normally in EX. No target cache, history table, speculative update,\n  pipeline stage, memory change or arithmetic modification is included.\n\n  Widen the internal EX-to-IF training index from six to eight bits.\n  Train exactly once per accepted legal nontrapping conditional branch,\n  using its registered instruction PC and actual resolved condition.\n  Invalid, held, wrong-path, illegal and trapping instructions never train